      "footnotes": [],
      "transcriber_notes": [
        "Reading order: the table reads across each printed line, left pair then right pair, and then down to the next line; read that way the 26 dates form one chronological series (1381, 1412, 1580, 1662, 1668, 1710, 1711, 1736, 1743, 1753, ,, , 1760, ,, , ,, , 1761, 1762, 1790, 1792, 1812, ,, , ,, , 1842, 1868, 1881, 1882, 1885). A ditto mark ',,' therefore repeats the year of the entry just before it in that order: the left-hand ',,' on line 6 is 1753, the ',,' marks on line 7 are both 1760, and the ',,' marks on lines 10 (right) and 11 (left) are 1812. So 1753 has two figures, 1760 three and 1812 three; 1710 has two figures on one line.",
        "Right block, 1710: two figures printed on one line, '23,312,200 & 27,241,129', kept as one cell.",
        "The year '1882' (last row of left block) is slightly damaged in the print but reads 1882."
      ],
      "context": "The table is on printed p. 2 under \"Area and Population\" in Chapter I. The chapter opens (p. 1) by warning that a census by Western methods \"has never yet been attempted in China\" and that estimates of the population \"have varied to an extraordinary degree\"; even the 1910 Minchengpu (Ministry of the Interior) census, the \"nearest approach to a reliable estimate\", \"can only be regarded as approximate\". The book introduces the table as \"the estimated population at various dates (obtained from various sources and abstracted chiefly from 'The Middle Kingdom')\", naming no source for any single figure. It is read across each line and then down, which puts the dates in order; the ditto marks (,,) repeat the year before them, so the book gives two estimates for 1710 and 1753 and three each for 1760 and 1812. The \"latest official estimates\" promised in the same sentence follow as the province table on p. 3.",
      "id": "p0041_3",
      "scans": [
        {
          "label": "scan leaf 41",
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/china-year-book-1912/page/n41/mode/1up"
        }
      ]
    }
